WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:03.853
You don't need to know any maths to follow this.

00:00:03.853 --> 00:00:07.651
A computer model is a system that learns patterns from examples.

00:00:07.651 --> 00:00:10.758
A transformer is one way of building that system.

00:00:10.758 --> 00:00:14.211
It can help with writing, translation and many other tasks.

00:00:14.211 --> 00:00:18.700
In this lab, we use a tiny one to choose Ava's next move.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.975
Let's use a simple example: the cat sat on the ...

00:00:23.975 --> 00:00:25.420
What might come next?

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:26.865
Mat is one possibility.

00:00:26.865 --> 00:00:33.008
A text-generating transformer learns to predict the next small piece of text from the pieces before it.

00:00:33.008 --> 00:00:36.982
Repeating that process can produce a sentence, then a whole answer.

00:00:36.982 --> 00:00:39.150
It can also produce something wrong.

00:00:40.450 --> 00:00:45.859
Before the model can work, the input is broken into small pieces called tokens.

00:00:45.859 --> 00:00:48.177
Think of them as labelled tiles.

00:00:48.177 --> 00:00:51.655
We show whole words here to keep things clear.

00:00:51.655 --> 00:00:55.905
Real text tokens can also be parts of words or punctuation.

00:00:55.905 --> 00:01:01.700
The model works with these pieces, rather than seeing a sentence the way you do.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:04.283
Computers calculate with numbers.

00:01:04.283 --> 00:01:07.811
So each token gets a pattern of numbers, called an embedding.

00:01:07.811 --> 00:01:11.019
Imagine a little set of sliders attached to each tile.

00:01:11.019 --> 00:01:12.302
Training adjusts those numbers.

00:01:12.302 --> 00:01:16.151
They help the model work with patterns in how pieces are used.

00:01:16.151 --> 00:01:20.000
The sliders in this picture are illustrations, not measurements from a model.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:22.029
Order matters.

00:01:22.029 --> 00:01:26.768
The dog chased the cat is different from the cat chased the dog.

00:01:26.768 --> 00:01:28.591
The pieces alone aren't enough.

00:01:28.591 --> 00:01:31.872
The model also receives information about each piece's position.

00:01:31.872 --> 00:01:37.340
In our tiny transformer, a learned position pattern is added to the token's number pattern.

00:01:37.340 --> 00:01:41.350
Now it has information about both the piece and its place.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:43.687
Next comes attention.

00:01:43.687 --> 00:01:48.872
For each piece, the model calculates how much information to take from other available pieces.

00:01:48.872 --> 00:01:52.675
In our sentence, earlier words can help with what comes next.

00:01:52.675 --> 00:01:57.169
The connections depend on the input and what training has taught the model.

00:01:57.169 --> 00:02:02.700
For this kind of text generation, it can't peek at words that haven't been supplied yet.

00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:09.920
Several attention heads let the model form different mixtures of information at the same time.

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:13.077
Think of several highlighters over the same sentence.

00:02:13.077 --> 00:02:15.445
Each can highlight a different pattern.

00:02:15.445 --> 00:02:19.786
We don't give one a permanent job, like grammar or walls.

00:02:19.786 --> 00:02:22.154
Their patterns are learned, then combined.

00:02:22.154 --> 00:02:26.100
These coloured connections are teaching pictures, not recorded attention scores.

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:31.392
After attention, another small network changes each piece's number pattern.

00:02:31.392 --> 00:02:34.152
Its technical name is a feed-forward network.

00:02:34.152 --> 00:02:38.094
Think of it as refining the information attention has gathered.

00:02:38.094 --> 00:02:42.431
It works on each piece separately, using the same learned calculation.

00:02:42.431 --> 00:02:44.796
It doesn't contain a hand-written answer.

00:02:44.796 --> 00:02:47.950
Its settings and internal numbers affect the transformation.

00:02:49.250 --> 00:02:52.961
Attention and refinement sit inside a transformer layer.

00:02:52.961 --> 00:02:59.456
Side connections carry earlier information forward, and balancing steps help keep the numbers manageable.

00:02:59.456 --> 00:03:04.094
Several layers repeat this processing, using their own learned settings.

00:03:04.094 --> 00:03:07.342
Later layers receive the earlier layers' results.

00:03:07.342 --> 00:03:14.300
More layers mean more computation and more to learn, but they don't guarantee better answers.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:21.238
At the end, the model turns the processed information into scores for possible next tokens.

00:03:21.238 --> 00:03:26.501
A text system can select one, add it to the input, and predict again.

00:03:26.501 --> 00:03:28.756
Different systems use different selection rules.

00:03:28.756 --> 00:03:31.388
Even a strong preference can be wrong.

00:03:31.388 --> 00:03:36.650
These example words illustrate the process; they aren't results from a trained language model.

1
00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:39.685
The architecture is the machinery.

2
00:03:39.685 --> 00:03:44.326
Training adjusts its internal numbers, called weights, using examples and a measure of

3
00:03:44.326 --> 00:03:44.683
error.

4
00:03:44.683 --> 00:03:48.253
Using those weights to make a prediction is called inference.

5
00:03:48.253 --> 00:03:51.823
In this lab, Model lets you inspect a fixed architecture.

6
00:03:51.823 --> 00:03:53.251
Training changes its weights.

7
00:03:53.251 --> 00:03:55.750
Playground uses them without teaching the model.

8
00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:00.315
Ava turns world information into predictions of moves.

9
00:04:00.315 --> 00:04:03.531
Chatbot turns a short story into an answer.

10
00:04:03.531 --> 00:04:07.550
Device messaging turns a simulated event history into a report.

11
00:04:07.550 --> 00:04:10.363
They are different applications of learned prediction.

12
00:04:10.363 --> 00:04:15.588
Start in any Playground, change its practice in Training, then test what happened.

13
00:04:15.588 --> 00:04:18.000
The model can still make mistakes.
